From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 19:35:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: G16: optionally bump the submit generation
 cells before a doorbell

On the J604, macOS 26B123 (with its own GPU firmware) was traced as a
guest under aura's hypervisor, recording every access to the GPU's
register window and mailbox. Before each submit doorbell the host
reads the firmware's mirror at SGX +0xd64000 + 2 * cell, reads an
8-bit generation cell on the host-owned page SGX +0xd60000 and writes
it back plus one, then rings. The cells, J604-measured in that trace:
pipe 0 bumps +0x20 then +0x00, pipe 1 +0x20, pipe 2 +0x40, pipe 8
+0x30 then +0x10, pipe 9 +0x30, pipe 0xa +0x50. This driver bumps none
of them; it uses cell +0x00 only for the device-control generation.

g16_subgen = 1 bumps the cells listed in g16_subgen_cells, in order,
right before that pipe's doorbell. The list is "pipe:cell[+cell],..."
for our pipes 0 (TA), 1 (FS) and 2 (compute), e.g. "2:0x40,1:0x20".
Cells must be 4-aligned and below 0x2000, and cell 0 is refused
because it is the device-control generation of the protocol this
driver speaks. A failed access is counted and the doorbell still goes
out. aurora-g16/stats gains a subgen line: bumps and how many found
the firmware's mirror caught up, per pipe.

The trace came from macOS's firmware, not the one this driver loads,
so the map is a candidate to test, not a qualified protocol.
g16_subgen defaults to 0 and the list to none, which keeps the
doorbell path unchanged.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/asahi/g16/engine.rs |  32 +++++
 drivers/gpu/drm/asahi/g16/params.rs | 186 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 drivers/gpu/drm/asahi/g16/poll.rs   |  12 ++
 drivers/gpu/drm/asahi/g16/regs.rs   |  16 +++
 4 files changed, 246 insertions(+)

